For the mean free path, use:

\[
\lambda=\frac{kT}{\sqrt{2}\pi d^2P}
\]

For oxygen, take molecular diameter \(d \approx 3.6\times 10^{-10}\,\text{m}\), \(T=25^\circ\text{C}=298\,\text{K}\).

At \(P=1\,\text{atm}=1.013\times10^5\,\text{Pa}\):

\[
\lambda \approx \frac{(1.38\times10^{-23})(298)}
{\sqrt{2}\pi(3.6\times10^{-10})^2(1.013\times10^5)}
\approx 7.0\times10^{-8}\,\text{m}
\]

So the first value is \(7.02\times10^{-8}\,\text{m}\).

Mean free path is inversely proportional to pressure:

\[
P_2=10^{-3}\,\text{torr}
\]

Since \(1\,\text{atm}=760\,\text{torr}\),

\[
P_2=\frac{10^{-3}}{760}\,\text{atm}
\]

Thus:

\[
\lambda_2=\lambda_1\frac{760}{10^{-3}}
=7.02\times10^{-8}\times 7.60\times10^5
\approx 5.3\times10^{-2}\,\text{m}
=5.3\,\text{cm}
\]

So the correct pair is:

\[
